{
  "format_version": "1.0",
  "description": "Adversarial cross-emitter matrix vectors targeting 10 Wycheproof-style categories (unicode-normalization, lone-surrogate, deep-nesting, integer-edge, empty-structure, mixed-type-array, sort-collision, zwj-rtl, escaped-control, boolean-integer-overlap). 26 vectors total, ids adv-01..adv-25 plus adv-ruby-lone-trailing. Same tuple-array record shape as canonicalization.json.",
  "rationale": "Corpus targets the 10 requested Wycheproof-style categories with 2-3 vectors each (25 base vectors, ids adv-01..adv-25) grounded in the tuple-array record shape from the existing canonicalization.json. Category coverage is skewed toward the fault modes with the highest cross-SDK divergence risk given prior findings: integer-edge gets 4 vectors (max-safe, min-safe, +1 past each) to isolate double-precision cutoffs from bignum SDKs, sort-collision uses supplementary vs. PUA and emoji vs. PUA key pairs to distinguish UTF-16-code-unit sorting from code-point sorting (the categorical failure mode most likely to break Swift/Python/Go/Rust at once), and lone-surrogate includes high-alone, low-alone, and reversed-pair variants because SDKs diverge on WHICH surrogate patterns their JSON encoder catches. Vector adv-ruby-lone-trailing (26th vector, category lone-surrogate) is the labeled version of a Ruby-json 2.7.2 finding: a lone trailing low surrogate \\uDE04 in a string body is silently accepted by Ruby's stdlib JSON.parse and stored as a 3-byte CESU-8/WTF-8 sequence rather than being rejected as unpaired UTF-16. Every other SDK in the matrix (JS, Python, Go, Java, Kotlin, Swift, PHP, C#, Rust x2) either rejects the input or Unicode-replaces the surrogate; Ruby is alone in preserving it, which breaks byte-for-byte cross-SDK canonical equality. Vectors preserve the 11-required-field shape and use aiInvocation/parties/decisionContextDigest as the injection surfaces so the matrix reuses the existing tuple-array plumbing without shape changes.",
